.TH IPTABLES\-ANALYZER 1 "2026-11-26" "0.7.1" "iptables-analyzer manual"
.SH NAME
iptables\-analyzer \- analyze iptables/ip6tables firewall rules for security issues
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iptables\-analyzer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B iptables\-analyzer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-trace\fR \fIPACKET\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]
.br
.B iptables\-analyzer
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-diff\fR \fIOLD\fR \fINEW\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iptables\-analyzer
examines iptables and ip6tables rules and reports security problems: chains
that accept by default, ports that are open to all sources, services that the
firewall does not protect, rules that never match, ports that the nat table
sends to a different port or to a different host (REDIRECT, port forwards,
Docker published ports, node ports of Kubernetes), Docker published ports that
bypass the INPUT rules, DOCKER\-USER and FORWARD rules that never match
published traffic, containers that other hosts reach by direct routing, and
rules that stop IPv6 connectivity.
.PP
The report starts with a score from 0 to 100 and a grade from A to F. Then it
lists the findings, the most severe first. Each finding names the rules that
cause it and says how to fix it. The tool does not change the firewall.
.PP
Run as root without a \fIFILE\fR, the tool reads the rules and the listening
sockets of the system. It also reads the facts that decide what the rules do:
the interfaces and their addresses, the IPv4 routes, the kernel settings
\fBip_forward\fR, \fBrp_filter\fR and \fBroute_localnet\fR, the contents of the
ipsets (\fBipset save\fR) and the list of nftables tables (\fBnft list
chains\fR). The tools \fBip\fR, \fBipset\fR and \fBnft\fR are used when they
are installed.
.PP
With files, the tool examines saved output of
.BR iptables\-save (8),
.BR ip6tables\-save (8)
and
.BR ss (8),
or a snapshot (see \fB\-\-snapshot\fR). The output of \fBiptables \-S\fR is
accepted too; the listings of several tables can follow one another in one
file. The tool finds out what each file holds: the save tools name themselves
in the first line of their output, and without that line the addresses in the
rules show the address family.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Input
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Read the IPv4 rules (iptables\-save output) from \fIFILE\fR. Use it when the
tool cannot tell what a file holds.
.TP
.BR \-\-file6 " \fIFILE\fR"
Read the IPv6 rules (ip6tables\-save output) from \fIFILE\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-services\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Read the listening sockets (output of \fBss \-tulnp\fR) from \fIFILE\fR.
Without the listening sockets, each open port is judged as if a service uses it.
.TP
.BR \-\-ipset\-file " \fIFILE\fR"
Read the contents of the ipsets (output of \fBipset save\fR) from \fIFILE\fR.
A rule that matches the source against a set of addresses or networks is then
evaluated like a rule with these sources. Without the contents, the report
names the set as a restriction that it cannot read.
.TP
.BR \-4 ", " \-\-ipv4\-only
Analyze only IPv4.
.TP
.BR \-6 ", " \-\-ipv6\-only
Analyze only IPv6.
.TP
.BR \-\-public " \fILIST\fR"
Ports that are open to all sources on purpose, for example
\fBtcp/80,tcp/443\fR. The findings for these ports are notes without a
deduction. An entry is \fBtcp/80\fR, \fB80/tcp\fR, \fBudp/53\fR, a range such
as \fBtcp/8000\-8100\fR, or a number (TCP).
.SS Queries
.TP
.BR \-\-trace " \fIPACKET\fR"
Show the way of one packet through the rules in place of the report: the rules
that it matches in each chain, a new destination from the nat table, and the
verdict. \fIPACKET\fR is
.RS
.PP
.RS 4
\fIPROTOCOL SOURCE\fR[\fB:\fIPORT\fR] \fB\->\fR [\fIDESTINATION\fR]\fB:\fIPORT\fR [\fBin\fR \fIIFACE\fR] [\fBout\fR \fIIFACE\fR] [\fBstate\fR \fISTATE\fR]
.RE
.PP
for example \fB'tcp 203.0.113.5 \-> :22 in eth0'\fR. \fIPROTOCOL\fR is
\fBtcp\fR, \fBudp\fR, \fBicmp\fR or \fBicmpv6\fR; for ICMP, \fBtype\fR \fIN\fR
replaces the port. A destination without an address is an address of this
host. An IPv6 address with a port is written in brackets. \fISTATE\fR is
\fBNEW\fR (the default), \fBESTABLISHED\fR, \fBRELATED\fR, \fBINVALID\fR or
\fBUNTRACKED\fR.
.PP
The trace knows the marks that rules set on the way, and it judges a rate
limit for a first packet. When a rule can match or not (a set whose contents
were not read, a random share of the packets, an interface that was not
given), the trace goes on without the rule and lists how the packet ends when
the rule does match. If these ends differ, the verdict is not certain.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-\-expect " \fIVERDICT\fR"
With \fB\-\-trace\fR: exit with status 2 unless the verdict is \fIVERDICT\fR
(\fBaccept\fR, \fBdrop\fR or \fBreject\fR) and is certain.
.TP
.BR \-\-diff
Compare two inputs in place of the report. The two \fIFILE\fR arguments are
the old and the new input; each is the output of a save tool or a snapshot.
The output shows the two grades, the findings that are new, gone or changed,
the ports of the host whose sources changed, and the changes of the ports that
are sent to other hosts. With \fB\-\-diff\fR, \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR judges only the
findings that the new input adds, and \fB\-\-min\-grade\fR the grade of the new
input.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-\-html " \fIFILE\fR"
Also write an interactive report to \fIFILE\fR. The file is one HTML page that
loads nothing from the network. It also holds the rules and the recommended
baseline. A symbolic link at \fIFILE\fR is not followed.
.TP
.BR \-\-snapshot " \fIFILE\fR"
Also save all input to \fIFILE\fR: the output of the tools, the interfaces and
the kernel settings, as JSON. The tool reads such a file like the host itself,
so the analysis, \fB\-\-trace\fR and \fB\-\-diff\fR give the results of the
host on any system and without root. A snapshot is a complete input: it cannot
be used together with other rule files. A symbolic link at \fIFILE\fR is not
followed.
.TP
.BR \-\-json
Write the report as JSON. See \fBdocs/json.md\fR for the fields.
.TP
.BR \-\-rules
Also show the rules of each chain, with the findings that name them.
With \fB\-\-json\fR, add the rules to the report.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Also show the findings of severity INFO, the listening sockets and the
sandbox status.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print one line: the score, the grade and the number of findings.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-score\-only
Print only the score and the grade, for example \fB87 B\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-baseline "[=\fIFAMILY\fR]"
Print only the recommended baseline firewall in iptables\-restore format and
exit. \fIFAMILY\fR is \fB4\fR, \fB6\fR or \fBall\fR (the default). A single
family is printed alone so it can be redirected into a rules file. The value
must be attached with \fB=\fR. Without rule files and without root the baseline
is generic; otherwise it keeps the detected SSH port, lists other listeners as
commented rules and, on Docker hosts, adds DOCKER\-USER rules for published ports.
Apply it with \fBiptables\-restore \-\-noflush\fR, which keeps Docker's chains.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-color
Disable colored output. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR variable has the same effect.
.SS Result
.TP
.BR \-\-fail\-on " \fISEVERITY\fR"
Exit with status 2 when a finding has this severity or a higher one.
\fISEVERITY\fR is \fBlow\fR, \fBmedium\fR, \fBhigh\fR or \fBcritical\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-min\-grade " \fIGRADE\fR"
Exit with status 2 when the grade is worse than \fIGRADE\fR (\fBA\fR to
\fBD\fR), or when the grade is INCOMPLETE.
.SS Other
.TP
.BR \-\-sandbox " \fIMODE\fR"
Confine the process after it has read its input; see
.BR SECURITY .
\fIMODE\fR is \fBauto\fR (the default: apply what the system supports),
\fBstrict\fR (fail unless the whole sandbox is in effect) or \fBoff\fR.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The analysis is complete.
.TP
.B 1
An error: the input could not be read, or an option is not correct.
.TP
.B 2
The analysis is complete, and the result is below what \fB\-\-fail\-on\fR,
\fB\-\-min\-grade\fR or \fB\-\-expect\fR asks for. The output is written first.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Do not use colors.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width of the text report, when it is not that of the terminal. The report
uses 60 to 120 columns, and 100 columns without a terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Examine the rules and the listening sockets of this host:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save the input on one host and examine it on another:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-save > rules.v4
sudo ip6tables\-save > rules.v6
sudo ss \-tulnp > ss.txt
iptables\-analyzer rules.v4 rules.v6 ss.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write an interactive report, and tell the tool that the web ports are public:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-public tcp/80,tcp/443 \-\-html report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Stop a script when the rules have a high or critical finding:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iptables\-analyzer rules.v4 rules.v6 \-\-quiet \-\-fail\-on high || exit 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save a snapshot of a host, and examine it on a different system:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-quiet \-\-snapshot host.json
iptables\-analyzer host.json \-\-html report.html
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show what the rules do with one packet, and test that a port stays closed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iptables\-analyzer host.json \-\-trace \(aqtcp 203.0.113.5 \-> :8443 in eth0\(aq
iptables\-analyzer rules.v4 \-\-trace \(aqtcp 203.0.113.5 \-> :5432\(aq \-\-expect drop
.EE
.RE
.PP
Refuse a change of the rules that adds a finding of medium or higher severity:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
iptables\-analyzer \-\-diff deployed.v4 proposed.v4 \-\-quiet \-\-fail\-on medium
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the recommended baselines to files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-baseline=4 > baseline.v4
sudo iptables\-analyzer \-\-baseline=6 > baseline.v6
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Rule files, snapshots, saved
.BR ss (8)
output and the live tool output are untrusted input: comments, the names of
chains, interfaces, ipsets and nftables tables, and process names are chosen
by whoever controls the firewall or runs a process. The analyzer therefore:
.IP \(bu 2
reads at most 64 MiB of rules, of a snapshot or of the output of one tool,
and 16 MiB of
.BR ss (8)
output;
.IP \(bu 2
in live mode, runs only root-owned, not group- or world-writable
.BR iptables\-save ,
.BR ip6tables\-save ,
.BR ss ,
.BR ip ,
.BR ipset " and " nft
from /usr/sbin, /usr/bin, /sbin or /bin (PATH is a fallback with the same
checks), with an environment that contains only PATH and LC_ALL=C. Each of
these commands only reads;
.IP \(bu 2
after reading its input, drops root privileges to uid and gid 65534, sets
no_new_privs, and enforces a Landlock domain that denies all file access,
execution, TCP and UDP, abstract UNIX sockets and signals (as far as the
kernel's Landlock ABI supports them). Parsing, analysis and output run in this
sandbox; the files for \fB\-\-html\fR and \fB\-\-snapshot\fR are opened before
it. Landlock needs
Linux 5.13 or later with landlock in the lsm= boot parameter; binaries built
with cgo need Linux 7.0 (Landlock ABI 8);
.IP \(bu 2
shows control characters, bidirectional overrides and invalid UTF\-8 in
input text as visible placeholders (\fB^[\fR, \fB<U+202E>\fR,
\fB<0xFF>\fR);
.IP \(bu 2
quotes every value from the ruleset in the commands of a fix, so that copying
a command into a shell cannot run a command hidden in a rule comment;
.IP \(bu 2
writes text from the input into the HTML report only as text, and gives the
page a content security policy that allows no network access and no script
but its own.
.PP
The reports and the snapshots show the rules of the host. Keep them where only
the right persons can read them.
